      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">1. Origin & Mathematical Formulation of the SaaS Quick Ratio</h2>
        <p>
          First formalized by venture capitalist Mamoon Hamid during his tenure at Social Capital, the <strong>SaaS Quick Ratio</strong> adapts the classic corporate liquidity test (acid-test ratio) to recurring revenue economics. While classic accounting evaluates whether short-term assets cover short-term liabilities, the SaaS Quick Ratio evaluates whether top-line customer revenue additions provide sufficient thrust to overcome gravitational drag from churn.
        </p>
        <p>
          Let \( \Delta \text{MRR}_t \) represent the net change in monthly recurring revenue over period \( t \). The revenue waterfall is decomposed into four non-overlapping vectors:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1 text-slate-300">
          <li><strong>New MRR (\( \text{MRR}_{\text{new}} \)):</strong> Revenue gained from entirely new customer acquisitions.</li>
          <li><strong>Expansion MRR (\( \text{MRR}_{\text{exp}} \)):</strong> Additional recurring revenue gained from existing customers through upsells, cross-sells, or seat expansion.</li>
          <li><strong>Churned MRR (\( \text{MRR}_{\text{churn}} \)):</strong> Revenue lost from customers who cancelled all active contracts.</li>
          <li><strong>Contraction MRR (\( \text{MRR}_{\text{cont}} \)):</strong> Revenue lost from existing customers who downgraded their plan or reduced seat counts without fully churning.</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 font-mono text-xs my-4 text-slate-300">
          <p class="text-cyan-400 font-bold mb-1">// Standard SaaS Quick Ratio Formula</p>
          <p>$$ \text{Quick Ratio} = \frac{\text{MRR}_{\text{new}} + \text{MRR}_{\text{exp}}}{\text{MRR}_{\text{churn}} + \text{MRR}_{\text{cont}}} $$</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
