      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">3. Decomposing the Quick Ratio: Expansion vs Acquisition Independence</h2>
        <p>
          Two software companies can register identical Quick Ratios of 4.0 while possessing fundamentally divergent risk profiles. To reveal underlying dynamics, decompose the formula into two distinct coefficients:
        </p>

        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 font-mono text-xs my-4 text-slate-300">
          <p class="text-cyan-400 font-bold mb-1">// Decomposition into Acquisition and Expansion Ratios</p>
          <p>$$ \text{Quick Ratio} = \underbrace{\frac{\text{MRR}_{\text{new}}}{\text{MRR}_{\text{churn}} + \text{MRR}_{\text{cont}}}}_{\text{Acquisition Efficiency Ratio } (R_{\text{acq}})} + \underbrace{\frac{\text{MRR}_{\text{exp}}}{\text{MRR}_{\text{churn}} + \text{MRR}_{\text{cont}}}}_{\text{Expansion Efficiency Ratio } (R_{\text{exp}})} $$</p>
        </div>

        <p>
          Notice the critical implication:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-300">
          <li><strong>Scenario A (Acquisition-Heavy):</strong> \( R_{\text{acq}} = 3.5 \), \( R_{\text{exp}} = 0.5 \). The company is relying entirely on new logo generation. If paid acquisition channels saturate or costs increase, growth will instantly stall.</li>
          <li><strong>Scenario B (Net-Expansion Compounder):</strong> \( R_{\text{acq}} = 1.8 \), \( R_{\text{exp}} = 2.2 \). Here, \( R_{\text{exp}} > 1.0 \), meaning expansion alone outstrips gross churn! The company exhibits negative net churn and will grow indefinitely even if outbound sales completely stops.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">4. Relationship Between Quick Ratio, NRR, and Gross Revenue Churn</h2>
        <p>
          The Quick Ratio directly interfaces with Net Revenue Retention (NRR). Let \( \text{MRR}_{\text{start}} \) denote beginning MRR for a given period. We define:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1 text-slate-300">
          <li>Gross Churn Rate (\( g_c \)) = \( \frac{\text{MRR}_{\text{churn}} + \text{MRR}_{\text{cont}}}{\text{MRR}_{\text{start}}} \)</li>
          <li>Net Revenue Retention (\( \text{NRR} \)) = \( 1 + \frac{\text{MRR}_{\text{exp}} - (\text{MRR}_{\text{churn}} + \text{MRR}_{\text{cont}})}{\text{MRR}_{\text{start}}} = 1 + e - g_c \)</li>
          <li>New Logo Growth Rate (\( g_n \)) = \( \frac{\text{MRR}_{\text{new}}}{\text{MRR}_{\text{start}}} \)</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Substituting these dimensionless ratios into the Quick Ratio yields:
        </p>
        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 font-mono text-xs my-4 text-slate-300">
          <p>$$ \text{Quick Ratio} = \frac{g_n + (\text{NRR} - 1 + g_c)}{g_c} = \frac{g_n + \text{NRR} - 1}{g_c} + 1 $$</p>
        </div>
        <p>
          This proof confirms that minimizing gross churn \( g_c \) in the denominator exerts hyper-linear leverage over the Quick Ratio compared to simply purchasing more top-of-funnel \( g_n \).
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">5. Python Waterfall Modeling Engine: Quick Ratio & 36-Month Runways</h2>
        <p>
          Deploy this automated financial modeling script to compute historical Quick Ratios, diagnose cohort decay, and forecast ARR capitalization trajectories:
        </p>

<pre is:raw class="bg-slate-950 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-800 text-xs font-mono overflow-x-auto text-emerald-400">
import pandas as pd

def analyze_saas_quick_ratio(
    starting_mrr: float,
    new_mrr: float,
    expansion_mrr: float,
    churn_mrr: float,
    contraction_mrr: float
) -> dict:
    inflow = new_mrr + expansion_mrr
    outflow = churn_mrr + contraction_mrr
    
    if outflow <= 0:
        quick_ratio = float('inf')
    else:
        quick_ratio = inflow / outflow
        
    net_mrr_delta = inflow - outflow
    ending_mrr = starting_mrr + net_mrr_delta
    
    # Advanced metrics
    r_acq = new_mrr / outflow if outflow > 0 else float('inf')
    r_exp = expansion_mrr / outflow if outflow > 0 else float('inf')
    gross_churn_pct = (outflow / starting_mrr) * 100
    nrr_pct = ((starting_mrr + expansion_mrr - outflow) / starting_mrr) * 100
    
    tier = (
        "VENTURE_SCALE" if quick_ratio >= 4.0 else
        "SUSTAINABLE" if quick_ratio >= 2.0 else
        "LEAKY_BUCKET" if quick_ratio >= 1.0 else
        "CONTRACTION_SPIRAL"
    )
    
    return {
        "quick_ratio": round(quick_ratio, 2),
        "tier": tier,
        "net_mrr_delta": net_mrr_delta,
        "ending_mrr": ending_mrr,
        "r_acq_ratio": round(r_acq, 2),
        "r_exp_ratio": round(r_exp, 2),
        "gross_churn_pct": round(gross_churn_pct, 2),
        "nrr_pct": round(nrr_pct, 2)
    }

if __name__ == "__main__":
    report = analyze_saas_quick_ratio(
        starting_mrr=100000.0,
        new_mrr=18000.0,
        expansion_mrr=12000.0,
        churn_mrr=4500.0,
        contraction_mrr=1500.0
    )
    print(f"Quick Ratio: {report['quick_ratio']}x ({report['tier']})")
    print(f"Expansion Ratio: {report['r_exp_ratio']}x | NRR: {report['nrr_pct']}%")
</pre>
