      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">6. Top 4 Growth Degradation Traps & Remediation Runbook</h2>
        <ul class="space-y-3 text-slate-300">
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white">1. Aggressive Sales Discounting Inflating Initial New MRR:</strong> Offering 50% first-year discounts artificially boosts New MRR while guaranteeing catastrophic churn and contraction at Year-2 renewal. <em>Remedy:</em> Eliminate unapproved custom discounts; structure multi-year contracts with indexation caps.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white">2. Blurring Product-Market Fit with Customer Success Rescues:</strong> Pouring CS human hours into manually saving accounts that do not need the core product masks poor product-market fit. <em>Remedy:</em> Segment Quick Ratios by ICP (Ideal Customer Profile) to identify low-efficiency customer cohorts.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white">3. Ignoring Contraction MRR in Internal Reporting:</strong> Reporting only logo churn while ignoring downgrades obscures revenue erosion. <em>Remedy:</em> Report full gross revenue destruction (Churn + Contraction) weekly to company leadership.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white">4. Misaligned Sales Compensation Structures:</strong> Paying commissions exclusively on new logo bookings incentivizes signing high-churn accounts. <em>Remedy:</em> Institute commission clawbacks if customers churn within 12 months, and reward expansion bookings equally with new bookings.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 my-6">
          <div class="border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-4 bg-slate-900/40">
            <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-white mb-2">How frequently should SaaS leadership calculate the Quick Ratio?</h3>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
              While monthly calculations provide operational telemetry for growth teams, quarterly rolling averages (or trailing 3-month trailing sums) prevent single-month contract renewals or seasonal spikes from distorting strategic governance.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-4 bg-slate-900/40">
            <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-white mb-2">Does an enterprise SaaS business with annual billing use the Quick Ratio?</h3>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
              Yes. Enterprise companies simply normalize Annual Contract Value (ACV) or Annual Recurring Revenue (ARR) into an ARR Quick Ratio: \( (\text{New ARR} + \text{Expansion ARR}) / (\text{Churn ARR} + \text{Contraction ARR}) \). The same 4.0x efficiency threshold applies.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-4 bg-slate-900/40">
            <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-white mb-2">Can early-stage pre-revenue or pre-$500k ARR startups rely on this metric?</h3>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
              In early pre-$500k ARR stages, small absolute dollar numbers create extreme volatility in the Quick Ratio (e.g., losing one $2,000 customer might cause the ratio to plunge). Early-stage founders should focus primarily on qualitative ICP retention and logo survival before tracking mathematical ratios at scale.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

    </div>
  </article>

  <script is:inline>
    function updateQR() {
      const newMrr = parseFloat(document.getElementById('qr-new').value) || 0;
      const expMrr = parseFloat(document.getElementById('qr-exp').value) || 0;
      const churnMrr = parseFloat(document.getElementById('qr-churn').value) || 0;
      const conMrr = parseFloat(document.getElementById('qr-con').value) || 0;

      const inflow = newMrr + expMrr;
      const outflow = churnMrr + conMrr;
      const ratio = outflow > 0 ? (inflow / outflow) : 0;

      document.getElementById('out-inflow').innerText = '$' + inflow.toLocaleString() + ' / mo';
      document.getElementById('out-outflow').innerText = '$' + outflow.toLocaleString() + ' / mo';
      document.getElementById('out-ratio').innerText = ratio.toFixed(2) + 'x';

      const tierEl = document.getElementById('out-tier');
      if (ratio >= 4.0) {
        tierEl.innerText = 'VENTURE SCALE';
        tierEl.className = 'text-base font-bold text-emerald-400 font-mono mt-1';
      } else if (ratio >= 2.0) {
        tierEl.innerText = 'HEALTHY MID-MARKET';
        tierEl.className = 'text-base font-bold text-cyan-400 font-mono mt-1';
      } else if (ratio >= 1.0) {
        tierEl.innerText = 'LEAKY BUCKET';
        tierEl.className = 'text-base font-bold text-amber-400 font-mono mt-1';
      } else {
        tierEl.innerText = 'CONTRACTION SPIRAL';
        tierEl.className = 'text-base font-bold text-rose-500 font-mono mt-1';
      }
    }

    document.getElementById('qr-new')?.addEventListener('input', updateQR);
    document.getElementById('qr-exp')?.addEventListener('input', updateQR);
    document.getElementById('qr-churn')?.addEventListener('input', updateQR);
    document.getElementById('qr-con')?.addEventListener('input', updateQR);
  </script>
</Layout>
